    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n76305" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀψιᾰνὸς</span></b> 
		<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λίθος, ὁ</span>, a
		black stone, perh. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>obsidian</i>, Pliny's <i>obsiana</i> (neut. pl.), <i><span class="en">
				<u>HN</u>
				36.196
			</span></i>, cf. <i><span class="en">
				<u>Peripl.M.Rubr.</u>
				5
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Orph.
				<u>L.</u>
				285
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Cyran.
				26
			</span></i>: written 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀψειανόν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					<u>PLeid.X.</u>
					36
				</span></i>
			
			<i><span class="en">
				B.
			</span></i>; [<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὀψ]ειανόν</span> dub. in <i><span class="en">
				<u>Supp.Epigr.</u>
				2.776
			</span></i><i><span class="en">
				 i 15
			</span></i> (Syria, <span class="entity">iii A. D.</span>). (Named from its discoverer
			Obsius, v.
			<i><span class="en">
				Plin.
			</span></i> l. c.; <i>obsidiana</i> is f.l. ibid.; prob.
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ᾱ</span> by nature.)</div>
